.TH EUCLID-CLI-EKV-SCAN 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ekv-scan \- read a table's items, a page at a time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ekv scan
.BR \-\-table | \-t
.I TABLE
[\fB\-\-page\-size\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-page\-index\fR \fIN\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads a table's items in key order, without regard to their key, and
says how many the table holds in total.
.PP
It reads everything, which is what a scan is. Use it to enumerate,
export, migrate or debug a table, and
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-query (1)
to serve requests: a scan's cost grows with the table, a query's with
the answer.
.PP
Items come back ordered by partition key and then sort key, so paging
through a quiet table sees each item once. A table being written to
while it is paged through can still repeat an item or miss one \-
paging is by page size and index rather than by a cursor, and inserts
move items between pages.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table " " \fITABLE\fR
Table to scan. Required.
.TP
.BR \-\-page\-size " " \fIN\fR
Most to return. Without it, every item in the table, which for a large
one is a large answer.
.TP
.BR \-\-page\-index " " \fIN\fR
Zero-based page, applied when a page size is given.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The items are printed, with how many came back and how many there are.
.TP
.B 1
No such table, or the request could not be sent.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ekv scan --table suppliers

# Export a table, a thousand at a time
page=0
while true; do
    got=$(euclid-cli ekv scan -t suppliers --page-size 1000 --page-index $page)
    [ "$(echo "$got" | jq '.count')" -eq 0 ] && break
    echo "$got" | jq -c '.items[]' >> suppliers.ndjson
    page=$((page + 1))
done
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ekv (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-query (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekv-delete-table (1)
